menu "System timer"

config TIMER_ARM_SYSTICK
    bool "Arm SysTick"
    depends on CPU_CORTEX_M
    help
      Clock event device on the Arm SysTick timer, counting CPU cycles. It
      stops in deep sleep.

config TIMER_QEMU
    bool "QEMU"
    help
      Clock source on the 1 kHz counter of the QEMU RTC.

config TIMER_NRF5_RTC
    bool "Nordic nRF5 RTC"
    select CLK
    help
      Clock source and clock event device on RTC1, counting the 32.768 kHz
      low-frequency clock. It keeps running in every sleep mode.

config TIMER_SF32LB_LPTIM
    bool "SiFli SF32LB LPTIM"
    select CLK
    help
      Clock source and clock event device on LPTIM1, counting clk_rtc, the
      calibrated LRC10 or the 32 kHz crystal. It keeps running and wakes the
      CPU up in deep sleep.

config TIMER_STM32_RTC
    bool "STM32F4 RTC"
    help
      Clock source and clock event device on the STM32F4 RTC: the calendar
      and its 256 Hz sub-second counter. Alarm A or the wakeup timer raises
      the clock events; both keep running and wake the CPU up in STOP mode.

config TIMER_STM32_RTC_WUT
    bool "STM32F4 RTC wakeup timer as the clock event"
    depends on TIMER_STM32_RTC
    help
      Raise the kernel's clock events with the RTC wakeup timer, in steps
      of 61 us. The kernel sets it up again on most ticks, with interrupts
      locked for about 60 us each time. With it on, snowy loses bytes of
      the CC2564's init script replies when Bluetooth is restarted. Off,
      alarm A raises the events (SysTick on the emulated SoC, which then
      cannot use STOP).

config TIMER_STM32_RTC_ALARM
    bool "STM32F4 RTC alarm A as the clock event"
    depends on TIMER_STM32_RTC && !TIMER_STM32_RTC_WUT && !SOC_STM32F4_EMULATED
    default y
    help
      Raise the kernel's clock events with alarm A and its sub-second
      match, in steps of the 256 Hz clock source; an event is at least
      three steps (11.7 ms) away. The emulator does not model the
      sub-second match.

config TIMER_STM32_RTC_WUT_PERIODIC
    bool "Leave the wakeup timer running between events of the same period"
    depends on TIMER_STM32_RTC_WUT
    default y
    help
      The kernel asks for an event every tick. Off, each request stops the
      timer and writes it again, which takes about 60 us with interrupts
      locked.

config TIMER_STM32_RTC_WUT_MIN_COUNT
    int "Shortest clock event, in wakeup timer counts (1/16384 s)"
    depends on TIMER_STM32_RTC_WUT
    default 16
    help
      16 is one kernel tick at 1024 Hz; 64 is one step of the 256 Hz clock
      source, below which the kernel sees no time pass.

config TIMER_POSIX
    bool "POSIX"
    help
      Clock source and clock event device on the host monotonic clock.

endmenu
